<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">People's Seimas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1940">1940</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as People’s Seimas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The People's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seimas">Seimas</a> was the parliament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> elected on 14–15 July 1940 under Soviet occupation, in a vote with a single list of Communist-approved candidates. On 21 July it proclaimed Lithuania a Soviet socialist republic and asked to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, which admitted it on 3 August 1940; the assembly then became the Supreme Soviet of the Lithuanian SSR.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The German-Soviet Boundary and Friendship Treaty assigned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> to the Soviet sphere of influence, and the Soviet-Lithuanian Mutual Assistance Treaty gave Lithuania part of the Vilnius Region in exchange for Soviet bases. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> was winning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-france">Battle of France</a>, Soviet propaganda accused Lithuania of abducting soldiers from those bases. On 14 June 1940 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> issued an ultimatum demanding a pro-Soviet government and passage for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> troops; Lithuania accepted, and the Red Army crossed the border unopposed the next day, with Vladimir Dekanozov sent to supervise the annexation.</p><p>President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antanas-smetona">Antanas Smetona</a> fled in protest, handing his powers to Prime Minister Antanas Merkys, who then declared himself president and appointed a pro-Soviet People's Government under the journalist Justas Paleckis. Paleckis, acting president after Merkys resigned, dissolved the Fourth Seimas on 27 June and on 1 July called elections for a new parliament.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> emerged from underground with 1,500 members, and the government formed the Union of the Working People of Lithuania, ostensibly a popular front, whose single list offered 79 candidates for 79 seats; no other organisation could take part. On 11 and 12 July the Soviet authorities arrested leading figures of the old regime and deported some of them. Voters had their passports stamped after voting. Official results gave a turnout of 95 percent and the Union list over 99 percent of the vote, a figure announced before the polls closed, while surviving records show many invalid ballots, some marked with anti-Soviet slogans.</p><p>Meeting on 21 July, the assembly passed by acclamation its one resolution, and a delegation of twenty, among them Paleckis, Petras Cvirka and Salomėja Nėris, each paid 5,000 litas for their signatures, carried it to the Supreme Soviet of the USSR. The People's Seimas also adopted a constitution closely copied from the Soviet Constitution of 1936.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Lithuanian and Western sources regard these proceedings as a cover giving the forcible occupation an illusion of constitutional legitimacy, while Soviet sources presented the election as the culmination of a socialist revolution carried out by the Lithuanian people themselves. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> declared independence in 1990 it held that the acts of the People's Seimas violated Lithuanian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a>, that Merkys' seizure of the presidency had been unconstitutional since Smetona never resigned, and that it was reasserting an independence that legally still existed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “People's Seimas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rigged elections produced a <b>People’s Seimas</b> that proclaimed the Lithuanian Soviet Socialist Republic, admitted to the Soviet Union on 3 August 1940.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Republic of Lithuania · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>On 21 July the <b>People's Seimas</b> voted to make Lithuania the Lithuanian Soviet Socialist Republic, and the Supreme Soviet approved its annexation on 3 August 1940.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania-1918-1940">Republic of Lithuania (1918-1940) · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>A rigged election produced a <b>People's Seimas</b> that requested admission to the Soviet Union, and Lithuania was annexed in August 1940, with Vilnius, regained in 1939, as its capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/lithuanian-soviet-socialist-republic">Lithuanian Soviet Socialist Republic</a></li></ul></section>
